# Baseline: let Git decide what is text, normalise it to LF in the repository,
# and check it out as LF on every platform including Windows. This keeps the
# repo free of CRLF churn when the same file is edited on Windows and Linux,
# and means CI runners see exactly what was committed.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit LF rules, kept from before the baseline was added. These are already
# implied by the line above, but they are the cases where a CRLF ending breaks
# something outright rather than merely creating diff noise, so they stay
# written down: a CRLF shebang makes a container entrypoint fail to execute
# (joshua/database/entrypoint.sh is covered by *.sh).
*.sh text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.conf text eol=lf
docker-compose.yml text eol=lf
*.sql text eol=lf

# Genuinely binary: never run end-of-line conversion over these, and do not try
# to diff them as text. Without this, text=auto's heuristic is all that stands
# between a byte sequence that looks textual and a corrupted file.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
